    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the sum of the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the equation in standard quadratic form:**\n   The given equation is \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\). We can expand and rearrange this equation to standard quadratic form.\n\n   \\[\n   (x - 5)^2 = 9\n   \\]\n\n   Expanding the left side:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 10x + 25 = 9\n   \\]\n\n   Subtract 9 from both sides to set the equation to zero:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 10x + 25 - 9 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying the left side:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 10x + 16 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the coefficients of the quadratic equation:**\n   The quadratic equation is now in the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -10\\), and \\(c = 16\\).\n\n3. **Use the sum of the roots formula:**\n   For a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\), the sum of the roots is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\).\n\n   Here, \\(a = 1\\) and \\(b = -10\\), so the sum of the roots is:\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{-10}{1} = 10\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
